15.2  Kernel modules

TL;DR
  - Most drivers are modules: pieces of the kernel loaded when the hardware that
    needs them shows up.
  - lsmod lists the loaded ones, modinfo describes one, modprobe loads one with
    what it depends on.
  - Module options and blacklists go in a .conf file in /etc/modprobe.d/.

      lsmod | sort -k2 -n | tail
      modinfo snd_hda_intel | head
      echo 'blacklist pcspkr' | sudo tee /etc/modprobe.d/nobeep.conf

On your machine
  Loaded    4 modules; biggest: nvidia 53.7 MiB, nvidia_modeset 1.5 MiB, iwlwifi
            476.0 KiB, snd_hda_intel 60.0 KiB
  Tainted   P: proprietary module loaded
            O: out-of-tree module loaded

Pitfalls
  - Modules are built for one kernel version. After a kernel update, modules
    from outside the kernel (NVIDIA, VirtualBox) must be rebuilt: DKMS does it
    for you.
  - A "tainted" kernel isn't broken: it runs code its developers can't debug,
    such as a proprietary module. Kernel developers usually ignore bug reports
    from tainted kernels.
  - blacklist only stops automatic loading: the module can still load by hand or
    as a dependency. install NAME /bin/false blocks it completely.
  - With Secure Boot on, the kernel refuses unsigned modules (see 14.1).

Deep dive

  ▾ Who loads modules?

    When a device appears, the kernel announces it with a "modalias", a string
    that describes the hardware. udev hands it to modprobe, which loads the
    module that claims it. Modules that have no hardware to wait for can be
    listed in /etc/modules-load.d/ to load at every boot.

  ▾ What do the taint letters mean?

    /proc/sys/kernel/tainted is a set of flags; 0 means clean. The common ones:
    P a proprietary module, O a module built outside the kernel tree, E an
    unsigned module, W the kernel issued a warning, D the kernel hit a fatal
    error (an "oops"). The full list is in the kernel documentation, under
    "tainted kernels".

Related
  Commands  lsmod, modinfo, modprobe
  Paths     /proc/modules, /etc/modprobe.d/, /etc/modules-load.d/
  See also  idk 14.1 From firmware to init
  See also  idk 15.3 dmesg and the kernel log
